@NODE OVERVIEW "About AmiMAIL"
AmiMAIL is a native general-purpose IMAP/SMTP mail client for AmigaOS 3.2+.
Unlike AmiGmail, AmiMAIL is not restricted to Gmail and can be configured
for normal IMAP/SMTP providers.

AmiMAIL supports up to five independently configured mail accounts. The
interface is based on ReAction and encrypted network connections use AmiSSL.

The focus is classic Amiga operation, modest resource requirements and a
robust plain-text-oriented mail workflow.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE REQUIREMENTS "Requirements"
- AmigaOS 3.2 or newer
- 68020 or newer
- working TCP/IP stack
- AmiSSL 5
- IMAP/SMTP account at a supported mail provider

A generous Workbench stack is recommended. The supplied program icon uses a
stack size of 100000 bytes.

The Amiga date and time should be correct so TLS certificates can be
validated reliably.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE INSTALL "Installation"
Start the supplied Installer and select the existing drawer where AmiMAIL
should be installed.

After language selection, the Installer can optionally copy the whole
Setup/Notifications drawer to SYS:Prefs/Presets/Notifications.

AmiSSL 5 must already be installed correctly. The Installer does not replace
or update AmiSSL.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ACCOUNT "First start and accounts"
On first use, open Account settings and enter the information supplied by
your mail provider.

AmiMAIL can manage up to five accounts. Account settings show only actually
configured accounts as native ReAction tabs.

- "+" creates another account, up to a maximum of five.
- "-" removes the selected account. Its files are deleted only after Save;
  Cancel discards the change.
- "<" and ">" move the selected account by one configured position per
  click. The selected account stays active. Save keeps the new order;
  Cancel discards it. The same order is used for the account tabs in the
  main window and Signature window.
- At least one configured account remains.
- At least one account must have "Activate account" enabled.

An inactive account remains fully configured, but it does not fetch mail and
does not appear as a selectable account tab in the main window.

Important fields:

- Account name: a freely chosen short name for the account. It is used on
  account tabs. If no account name is set, the e-mail address is used as a
  fallback.
- Sender name: the visible name in the From: header, for example
  "John Smith <address@example.com>".
- E-mail address: the address used as the message sender.
- IMAP server, port, username and password.
- SMTP server, port and, if needed, separate SMTP credentials.

Server settings, credentials, system-folder mapping, fetch preferences and
notifications are stored separately for each account.

Email retrieval and notifications
---------------------------------
"Email retrieval:" groups the startup and periodic retrieval settings.
"Fetch mail at startup" controls only the initial retrieval. The separate
"Periodic fetch" checkbox enables automatic Inbox checks for this account.
Choose 1, 2, 5, 10, 15 or 30 minutes in the right-hand cycle/chooser field.
It is disabled while the periodic checkbox is off; the selected interval
is retained. Save applies the changes. Cancel leaves saved settings intact.
Existing configurations keep the previous five-minute interval.

Each active account uses its own interval, independent of the tab order.
Changing one interval does not reset the timers of unchanged accounts.
A busy connection or a modal dialog can delay a check; missed intervals
are not replayed as a burst of requests. Disabling an account stops its
periodic checks. Startup retrieval is independent of the chosen interval.

"Notifications:" labels the sound settings. "External:" labels the optional
Herald integration. The "?" button tests Herald without changing settings;
it has the same width as the sound-file "..." button above it.

AmiMAIL can use the same credentials for SMTP and IMAP, or retain separate
SMTP credentials.

Credentials are stored locally in protected form. AmiMAIL creates and uses a
local per-account key automatically. No master password is required at program
startup or when changing or saving an account.

If a very old encrypted account cannot be opened because its matching local
key is unavailable, its public settings remain available. In that case, only
the IMAP/SMTP password needs to be entered again before saving the account.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE SERVERS "IMAP/SMTP and TLS"
AmiMAIL supports direct TLS connections and, depending on the configured
service, STARTTLS. Required ports and TLS mode depend on your mail provider.

For Gmail, the tested configuration is IMAP 993 with direct TLS and SMTP 587
with STARTTLS. Other providers require their own settings.

When connecting, AmiMAIL tries all IPv4 addresses returned for the server.
TLS failures report the SSL error type, socket error and, where available,
negotiated TLS version and cipher.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE FOLDERS "Mapping system folders"
AmiMAIL is not tied to one provider's folder names. INBOX is the IMAP Inbox;
Sent, Trash and Drafts can be mapped to the folders used by your server.

Manual mapping is important for providers that use names such as Sent Items,
Trash, Deleted Messages or different Drafts folders.

The mapping affects draft storage, sent-message copies, deletion and the
related main-window actions.

For Gmail/Google Mail accounts, the non-selectable "[Gmail]" or
"[Google Mail]" namespace container is hidden when no custom visible
subfolders remain after the system folders have been mapped.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE MAINWINDOW "The main window"
The account tab bar is directly below the graphical header. Main actions such
as New Mail, Fetch, Reply and Delete are immediately below the tabs.

The tabs show active accounts only and use the configured Account name. If no
Account name is set, the e-mail address is used as a fallback. Their order is
the account order configured in Account settings.

The folder list is on the left. The message list is in the upper-right area
and the message preview is below it.

The message list uses the native ListBrowser vertical ReAction prop. The
preview is directly coupled to its ReAction scroller through BOOPSI. Both
support mouse-wheel scrolling.

The main window stays fully interactive while the Compose window is open.
Message selection, preview updates, manual fetch and network events continue
to be processed.

Main-window position and size are saved on normal exit. ReAction remains
responsible for the window backfill, allowing configured background patterns
to be used.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE READING "Reading and managing mail"
Select a message to read it in the lower preview area. AmiMAIL supports the
usual IMAP actions such as read/unread state, moving and deleting messages.

Delete moves the selected messages to the server's mapped Trash folder.
It does not permanently erase them. Empty Trash is the separate permanent
removal action. Check the system-folder mapping if the provider uses a
different Trash name.

AmiMAIL refreshes server capabilities after login and prefers UID MOVE.
Without MOVE, it uses COPY and marks the source deleted, followed by a
UID-specific EXPUNGE when UIDPLUS is available. It checks the source UID
before and after the move, rather than treating every server OK as proof
that a message was removed.

If neither MOVE nor UIDPLUS is available, the copy is kept in Trash and
the source is only marked deleted. A status message explains this deferred
removal. AmiMAIL never uses a mailbox-wide EXPUNGE for a single-message
Delete, since that could remove other messages marked by another client.
After a failed or unconfirmed move, reload before retrying.

Reply, Reply All and Forward are available from the Reply button and its
adjacent selection area. Clicking the arrow opens or closes the popup only
when the mouse button is released; the popup stays visible while the closing
click is held. Escape and clicking elsewhere still dismiss it.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE COMPOSE "Compose, reply and forward"
New Mail opens the Compose window with To, CC, BCC, Subject, message body
and attachments.

The configured Sender name is written as the visible name in the
RFC-compliant From: header. The SMTP envelope still uses only the plain
e-mail address.

Reply All filters the user's own address and duplicate recipients. Forward
creates a new message without prefilled recipients and can retain existing
attachments.

The Compose window position and size are saved. The same shared geometry is
used for replies, Reply All, forwards and editing drafts.

The main window remains active while composing.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE DRAFTS "Drafts"
Drafts can be stored on the IMAP server and reopened later.

To, CC, BCC, Subject, body text, thread references and attachments are
restored when editing a draft.

Saving first appends the replacement draft and only then removes the old
one. Sending removes the old draft only after SMTP delivery succeeds.
Cancelling leaves the server draft unchanged.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ATTACHMENTS "Attachments"
There is no fixed attachment-count limit. Outgoing attachments together may
contain at most 20 MiB (20,971,520 bytes) of original file data. MIME encoding
adds overhead; the complete received MIME message is limited to 32 MiB.

"Save attachments" opens a separate file-selection window. It lists each
name, whether it is an attachment or an embedded graphic, and the encoded
size in the original mail. Initially only regular attachments are selected.
Choose individual rows, Select All, None, Attachments only or Graphics only.
"Save selected" then asks for a destination drawer.

AmiMAIL remembers the last directory in which at least one attachment was
successfully saved. It is offered again for all accounts, including after
a restart. Cancelling before any file is saved leaves this default unchanged.
If the remembered directory is unavailable, the normal drawer selection is
used instead. The preference is stored in
ENVARC:AmiMail/attachment-drawer.state. If this cannot be written, the new
directory is remembered only for the running session until a later save
can persist it.

Existing destination files are never overwritten. A numeric suffix is added
on name collisions. Each export is decoded to a private temporary file in
the destination drawer and published only after successful completion.
Cancel stops the current export, removes that incomplete temporary file,
and leaves any files already saved intact.

MIME-embedded images remain separate under "Embedded graphics" in the
preview. External images referenced only by HTML URLs are never downloaded.
Attachments are preserved when forwarding or editing a supported draft.

Large-mail processing and transfers
----------------------------------
Incoming MIME data is spooled to disk instead of retained as a full RAM copy.
Only MIME metadata and a bounded text preview remain in memory. Scrolling,
read-only protection and URL/mail-address double-clicks are unchanged.
A long or unsupported text body does not prevent saving its attachments.

Before sending or saving a draft, AmiMAIL prepares a private .eml snapshot.
SMTP and the Sent-folder copy use that same snapshot. Uploads are streamed,
not assembled as an additional full-size MIME buffer in memory.
The bottom status row keeps messages on the left and shows a compact
percentage bar on the right. No transfer popup is opened. Only the current
account, folder and selected message can supply mail-transfer progress.
The status field, progress bar and X share the same height.
Loading a folder such as "All Mail" also shows progress for the selected
account and folder. It counts processed messages in the configured fetch
period, not transferred bytes. While the server search has not yet returned
the count, the percentage is blank. 100% requires the final acknowledged
fetch. The subsequent list rendering is not a separate progress stage.
Background checks for other accounts and folders stay silent.
For mail transfers the percentage refers to the current processing step or
attachment. The bar is cleared after completion or a context change.

The small X cancels only the displayed operation, when cancellation is safe.
Cancel is available during receive, indexing and upload. During preparation
it is disabled until a recovery copy is complete; it is also disabled while
waiting for the final server confirmation. Attachment export uses the same
status row. Navigation is temporarily locked during local file operations;
X or Escape cancels them. If fuelgauge.gadget is unavailable, a percentage
label is used instead of the bar. Closing AmiMAIL cancels network I/O and
waits for workers to finish before freeing their data.

Temporary data normally goes to PROGDIR:Spool on the program volume. To use
another writable disk drawer, set the global environment variable
AmiMAIL_SpoolDir to its path before starting AmiMAIL. Do not choose RAM: or
T: when the goal is to reduce RAM use. Allow enough free disk space for the
original mail, decoded draft attachments and the outgoing snapshot.

Successful temporary work is removed automatically. Failed/uncertain sends
or failed Sent copies retain the outgoing snapshot; the status message gives
its path. An incoming spool also contains IMAP framing and is not a plain
.eml export. Old job folders left by a crash are not deleted blindly.
A partially prepared outgoing file is marked as incomplete and must not be
resent as-is; its original temporary attachments are retained separately.

A lost final server confirmation means the result is UNKNOWN. Check the
server's Sent/Drafts folder and delivery before retrying, to avoid duplicate
mail. AmiMAIL never retries an uncertain send automatically. Old server
drafts are removed only after a confirmed replacement/save or SMTP send.
Recovery files can contain private mail and Bcc recipients; protect their
drawer and remove them manually only after the message has been recovered.

@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE HTMLMAIL "HTML mail"
AmiMAIL does not contain a full HTML browser. A clean text/plain part is
preferred when available, and its existing paragraphs and blank lines are
preserved.

HTML-only messages are converted locally to plain text. Paragraphs, line
breaks, lists and links are retained where practical.

CSS, style/script/head content, tracking pixels and other external content
are not loaded. Extremely long tracking-only URLs are omitted from the
preview.

HTML entities and numeric entities are decoded. UTF-8 characters are mapped
to useful Amiga output where practical, including the Euro symbol,
typographic quotation marks, apostrophes and en/em dashes. Emoji and
pictographs that cannot be represented directly are shown as "[Grafik]".

For HTML images, useful alt text is shown as plain text. Images without useful
alt text are represented as "[Grafik]"; hidden images and tracking pixels are
omitted.

For broken multipart/alternative messages whose supposed plain-text part is
actually generated CSS/HTML debris, AmiMAIL can automatically use the HTML
alternative and generate clean plain text from it.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE CONTACTS "Contacts and signature"
Edit -> Contact management... opens the local address book. Contacts can be
created, edited and deleted, and CSV or VCard files can be imported.

Contacts can be added to To, CC and BCC from a multi-selection list in the
Compose window.

Edit -> Signature... manages a separate signature for each active mail
account. The Signature window uses the same Account names and order as the
account tabs.

The signature belonging to the account being used is inserted automatically
in new messages, replies, Reply All, forwards and mailto: drafts.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE NOTIFY "Notifications and iconify"
AmiMAIL can play a configured notification sound for each active account
when new mail arrives. Sound preview uses the same playback path.

Playback uses the installed sound DataTypes in a separate process. An
external C:SoundPlayer command is not needed. The supplied 8SVX samples are
suitable notification sounds. WAV files require a matching installed
DataType for their encoding.

Only one notification/preview sound plays at a time. Further requests during
playback are skipped. Closing AmiMAIL stops the sound and waits for its
worker to release the audio resources. A missing completion signal is
limited by a two-minute watchdog; notifications are intended as short sounds.

The main window can be iconified. Network workers for active accounts,
periodic fetch and status handling continue in the background.

The current Inbox state is exposed during runtime through the AmiMAILStatus
environment variable.


Optional Herald notifications
----------------------------
Account settings contain "Herald notifications" and a "?" test button.
The option is stored separately for each account and is off by default.
The "?" button sends a separate test card using the current account name;
it does not enable the option or save changes to the account.

Herald must already be running. AmiMAIL neither installs nor starts Herald.
Communication uses native RexxMsg messages and rexxsyslib.library; RexxMast,
an ARexx script and C:HeraldSend are not required. Automatic notifications
silently skip an unavailable Herald. Test results appear in the account
window's status field, not in a requester.

Enabled accounts notify when an Inbox check detects new message UIDs after
its baseline has been established. This includes background accounts and an
iconified AmiMAIL. The initial unread snapshot, a UIDVALIDITY reset, opening
an existing message and reloading another folder do not trigger these cards.

New-mail cards always include a subject. A single new message shows the
account and "Subject:". Several new messages show their count and "Latest:"
with the subject of the newest arriving message. There is no separate
subject-display option. A missing subject is shown as "(No subject)".

Newest means the highest new non-deleted UID within this Inbox, not the
sender's Date header or the order in which the server returned messages.
AmiMAIL uses the already fetched headers; no extra message download is
needed. The subject is decoded for the local display and control characters
are removed. Long account names and subjects are shortened to fit Herald's
160-byte text limit. Subject text can therefore be visible outside AmiMAIL.

Account slots have separate IDs and groups; moving account tabs does not
change their notification identity. A newer unsent batch for the same
account replaces the pending one. The count is not an unread-mail total.

Herald cards use normal priority and NOSOUND, including tests. AmiMAIL's
existing notification sound remains independently configurable. Display
duration, grouping and Do Not Disturb behavior remain Herald preferences.
An accepted test means Herald accepted the command, not that a popup must
be visible despite those preferences. Cards do not activate AmiMAIL or
switch accounts when clicked in this first integration.

The existing status-row progress bar is unchanged and stays inside AmiMAIL.
See docs/FIX_2.1.0_HERALD.md for timeout behavior and the exceptional shutdown
case when Herald keeps an outstanding message without replying.
Subject and retrieval changes: docs/FIX_2.1.0_HERALD_SUBJECT_INTERVALS.md.

@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE INTEGRATION "mailto: and updates"
AmiMAIL supports mailto: URIs containing recipient, CC, BCC, Subject and
Body. If AmiMAIL is already running, the URI is handed to the existing
instance instead of starting a second mail session.

The GitHub update check runs asynchronously and at most once per program
start. An available release can be downloaded to RAM:; installation and
unpacking remain manual.

Browsers such as IBrowse can be configured to use AmiMAIL as an external
mailto: handler.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE KEYBOARD "Keyboard operation"
- Return/Enter confirms the primary OK/Apply action in requesters.
- Escape closes or cancels dialogs that provide a cancel action.
- TAB follows the ReAction focus order in input windows.
- The mouse wheel scrolls the message list and message preview.

The exact response of some keys also depends on the currently active
ReAction gadget.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE TROUBLE "Troubleshooting"
For connection errors, first compare server name, port, TLS mode and
credentials with the settings supplied by the mail provider.

- Verify TCP/IP connectivity and DNS resolution.
- Check AmiSSL 5 and the system date/time.
- Use a Google App Password when connecting to Gmail.
- For SSL_ERROR_SYSCALL or connection reset by peer, test the same server
  with openssl s_client.
- For Sent/Trash/Drafts problems, verify the system-folder mapping.

AmiMAIL provides detailed TLS diagnostics where possible. Include those
details together with TCP/IP stack, AmiSSL version and server configuration
in bug reports.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE

@NODE ABOUT "About / Copyright"
AmiMAIL 2.2.0 - native general IMAP/SMTP mail client for AmigaOS 3.2+.

© 2026 Andreas "Andiweli" Stürmer

AmiMAIL uses ReAction, IMAP, SMTP and AmiSSL and supports up to five
independently configured mail accounts.


@{"Back to contents" LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE
